  "query_preview": "..Today...Following the very active day yesterday, a \ndownturn in activity is in store for many. Much of the hi-res \nguidance indicates potential for more thunderstorms across Navajo \nand Apache county, however we are yet to see any deep convection \nas of 1300 MST. The continuous band of light showers and clouds \novernight into this morning may be hindering daytime \ndestabilization in this region. Thunderstorms with heavy rainfall \nwill still be possible towards the late afternoon especially if we\nsee better clearing. Further west, drier air is beginning to move\nin and storms are expected to stay isolated. There is a slight \nchance for showers to continue overnight across eastern AZ as \nanother shortwave passes.\n\nFriday through Sunday...A continued downturn in activity is \nexpected. A larger west coast trough is forecast to push inland on \nFriday-Saturday and introduce much drier air. A few showers and \nthunderstorms will still be possible across the AZ-NM border on \nFriday before drier air moves in. By Saturday and Sunday showers \nwill be extremely isolated to nonexistent as PWAT drops to below the \n25th percentile. The dry air also means cold morning lows. Many \nareas across the high country will see temperatures dip into the 30s \non Sunday morning, with some locations seeing the first freeze of \nthe year.\n\nMonday and beyond...Another west coast trough looks to approach \nearly next week. Uncertainty starts to increase considerably at this \npoint, but the general consensus among ensemble guidance is for \nshower chances to increase Monday-Tuesday",
